# intent spec

# AIDE HANDOFF

Cold-start protocol for agents reorienting on this project. This is a
snapshot of the current architectural intent and the anti-regression
invariants every build phase must hold. It is NOT historical commentary —
superseded design decisions are cut, not annotated.

---

## State summary (set 2026-04-27)

The four-section / three-artifact / extended-carve-out cycle has landed
end-to-end. Tests are 879 green project-wide; `tsc --noEmit` is exit 0.
The pipeline-is-code-only rule is encoded permanently in the orchestrator
command (`.claude/commands/aide.md`).

If you are reorienting cold and the user asks "what's the state?" — answer:
the brain layer's body grammar is four marker-bounded sections; the framework's
brain contract is three required entry-point artifacts; provisionBrain plans a
six-step pipeline; the bundled obsidian template ships obsidian-flavored content
across all four of its body sections under the explicit fourth carve-out in
intent #15; agent/command/skill prompts under `.claude/` are direct-edit only,
never pipelined.

---

## The architectural intent (what the specs say)

The brain layer is governed by the decisions below.

1. **`brain.aide` lives at `.aide/config/brain.aide`.** The `.aide/config/`
   directory is user-owned: scaffolded once at `aide_init` if absent, never
   overwritten by `aide_init` or `aide_upgrade` after that. The directory path
   itself is the ownership signal — install/upgrade tooling reads it, no
   per-file allowlist.

2. **The default scaffolded launcher is `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`.** The bundled
   `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper emits `npx @bitbonsai/mcpvault
   <brain-root-path>`. Hosts that want a different brain edit one file and run
   `npx aidemd-mcp sync`.

3. **Frontmatter schema = exactly two top-level fields:** `name` (a
   human-readable label the package never branches on) and `mcpServerConfig`
   (`command` + `args` that sync writes verbatim into `.mcp.json` under
   `mcpServers["brain"]`).

4. **Body schema = exactly four marker-bounded sections.** Sections in fixed
   order: `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, `research`. Eight markers total,
   four pairs:
   - `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`
   - `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`
   - `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`
   - `<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->`

   Marker tokens are lowercase, case-sensitive, byte-exact form. Field names on
   the parsed result are camelCase: `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`,
   `research` — no `Hub` suffix on any of them.

5. **Each consumer reads only its owned section:**
   - `tools/brain` (runtime brain entry-point tool) reads `prose` only,
     returns it byte-identical to callers.
   - `provisionBrain` reads `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` to
     scaffold the three entry-point artifacts on cold install.
   - `cli/sync` and `buildBrainState` read frontmatter only.
   - The agent-side `study-playbook` skill is minimal — it reads
     `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` (the on-disk artifact provisioned from
     the `studyPlaybook` body section), never the brain.aide body section
     directly.

6. **Entry-point artifact bytes flow from `brain.aide`, never from inline
   TypeScript constants.** Applies to all three required artifacts.

7. **The framework's brain contract = exactly three required entry-point
   artifacts:** `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` (from `playbook` body
   section), `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` (from `studyPlaybook` body
   section), `research/research.md` (from `research` body section).

8. **`provisionBrain` is a 6-step pipeline:** brain config → brain root
   directories → playbook entry-point → study-playbook entry-point → research
   entry-point → MCP entry. The new `buildStudyPlaybookStep` builder is a peer
   of `buildPlaybookStep` and `buildResearchStep` — same presence-only
   idempotency, same shape.

9. **Removed frontmatter fields:** `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
   `tools`. The parser actively rejects these.

10. **No code path branches on `name`.** It's a label.

11. **No introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args` for any purpose.**

12. **Boot reporter brain status states = four:** `ok`, `no-brain-aide`,
    `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`.

13. **`${...}` interpolation of frontmatter values into `mcpServerConfig.args`
    is supported** for advanced users. The default scaffold does NOT exercise
    this surface. Substitution lives ONLY in `mcpServerConfig.args` at
    install/sync time — NEVER in any body section at agent time.

14. **The MCP server never silently rewrites `.mcp.json`.** Sync is the only
    mutation surface.

15. **Storage-agnostic vocabulary across every surface this package authors.**
    The forbidden-token list — `vault`, `obsidian`, `note` (used as a synonym
    for "file"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and `hub` — must not appear in any
    user-facing prose surface NOR in code identifiers, comments, or string
    constants project-wide.

    **Four explicit carve-outs:**
    - The `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder/function name (describes
      bundled template content, not a dispatch axis).
    - The `.obsidian` directory heuristic comment in `brainRootExists` (an
      honestly-labeled implementation detail).
    - The legacy `obsidian` MCP key referenced inside migration branches (the
      key being migrated away from).
    - **The body section bytes of the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`**
      across all four sections (`prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`,
      `research`). Those bytes ship obsidian-flavored content because the
      bundled template IS the obsidian default; they become user-owned the
      moment they are scaffolded onto disk; hosts that wire a different brain
      edit the section bytes in their own `brain.aide`.

    The bundled-template-body-bytes carve-out is scoped exactly: it covers the
    bytes inside the template's body section literals only — NOT JSDoc, NOT
    identifiers, NOT parameter names, NOT function names in the template
    module's `index.ts`, NOT string constants outside the body section
    literals, NOT anti-example bullets in undesired-outcome blocks (those have
    their own anti-example carve-out), and NOT any other bundled template
    should one ever exist unless explicitly named as another connector-flavored
    default.

    Anti-example occurrences inside undesired-outcome blocks are also allowed —
    naming a forbidden token to forbid it is the mechanism, not a violation.

16. **Strict-failure migration for body schema changes.** Two pre-pivot shapes
    fail closed:
    - Heading-based bodies (`## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub`,
      no marker pairs) return `malformed-body` naming all eight missing markers.
    - Three-pair marker-bounded bodies (prose + playbook + research, no
      `aide-study-playbook-*` pair) return `malformed-body` naming the missing
      study-playbook markers specifically.

    Users hand-edit their `brain.aide`. No `aide_upgrade` carve-out
    auto-migrates the body.

17. **Agent-invokes-sync for the wiring loop, not user-invokes-sync.**

18. **The AIDE pipeline is for code only.** `.claude/agents/`,
    `.claude/commands/`, `.claude/skills/` are never pipelined; they are
    canonical agent / command / skill prompts and ARE the contract every
    pipeline phase works from. Wrapping them in `.aide` specs and re-deriving
    them through the pipeline is the inversion that produces broken cycles.
    Direct edits only on those surfaces. Per-command `.aide` specs are an
    anti-pattern. **Methodology docs and intent specs that DESCRIBE code
    contracts** (`.aide/docs/`, `.aide/intent.aide`, `.aide/config/brain.aide`)
    ARE pipeline-appropriate when their updates ride alongside a code
    amendment — they describe what the code does, so they evolve with the
    code.

---

## Anti-regression invariants future build phases must hold

These are the hard rules from `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.undesired plus
permanent additions from prior cycles.

- **Body schema is marker-bounded, not heading-bounded.** No code path may
  walk `^## .+$` boundaries to extract any body section.
- **No tolerant matching of marker variants.** Uppercase, mixed-case,
  stray-whitespace, missing-`aide-`-prefix variants are ALL rejected as
  malformed.
- **Bytes outside marker pairs are silently ignored.**
- **No transitional read path for pre-pivot files.** Both heading-based and
  three-section pre-pivot bodies fail closed as `malformed-body`.
- **No silent acceptance of marker-layout violations.**
- **Field-name regression class.** No `Hub` suffix on `prose`, `playbook`,
  `studyPlaybook`, or `research` field names. The safe-list is exactly those
  four camelCase names.
- **No forbidden-token leakage** in code identifiers, JSDoc, parameter names,
  function names, or string constants outside the four explicit carve-outs in
  intent #15.
- **`aide_info`'s outdated flag is not actionable in this repo.** This package
  IS the canonical source for every artifact under `.claude/agents/`,
  `.claude/commands/`, `.claude/skills/`, and `.aide/docs/`. The `outdated`
  array compares the host's `.aide/versions.json` against the manifest shipped
  with the npm package — but in THIS repo, the local files ARE what ships
  next. Any post-publish edit shows as "outdated" even though it is the new
  source of truth. Ignore the outdated array when working in this repo.
- **No code path branches on `name`.**
- **No introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args`.**
- **No silent acceptance of deprecated frontmatter fields.**
- **No server-side substitution in any body section.**
- **No silent `.mcp.json` mutation.**
- **No overwrite of files under `.aide/config/`** on subsequent `aide_init` /
  `aide_upgrade` runs.
- **No new frontmatter field whose only consumer is the parser's own
  validation.**
- **No inline TypeScript constants for entry-point artifact bytes.** Applies
  to all three required artifacts (`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
  `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, `research/research.md`) — bytes flow
  from `brain.aide` body sections via `parseBrainAide`, never from inline
  constants.
- **No CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under any name.** The framework's brain
  contract is exactly three required entry-point artifacts; brain-root
  CLAUDE.md is not one of them.
- **No backend-driver dispatch, no connector registry, no matcher-by-shape.**
- **No reading another consumer's owned body section.** Per-consumer ownership
  is strict: brain tool reads `prose` only; provisionBrain reads `playbook` /
  `studyPlaybook` / `research`; sync and boot reporter read frontmatter only.
  The agent-side `study-playbook` skill reads the on-disk artifact
  (`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`), not the brain.aide body section
  directly.
- **No hardcoded sync invocation in any skill or command prompt.**
- **No terminal-punt prose on the success path of any broken brain state.**
- **No re-call of `aide_info` (or any boot state read) after sync completes in
  the same session.**
- **The AIDE pipeline is for code only.** `.claude/agents/`,
  `.claude/commands/`, and `.claude/skills/` are never pipelined. Per-command
  `.aide` specs are an anti-pattern. (Permanent invariant encoded in the
  orchestrator command.)
- **The bundled-template-body-bytes carve-out (intent #15's fourth carve-out)
  is scoped to BYTES only.** Code that reads the carve-out as a license to
  introduce obsidian vocabulary in JSDoc, identifiers, parameter names,
  function names, or string constants outside the body section literals is a
  regression.

---

## Process discipline (permanent)

The orchestrator's contract treats the two methodology-mandated pause-for-
approval points as unconditional regardless of mode:

- After `aide-spec-writer` returns frontmatter (Stage 1) → present to user,
  wait for explicit approval before delegating synthesis or proceeding past
  the spec.
- After `aide-architect` returns `plan.aide` (Stage 4) → present to user,
  wait for explicit approval before delegating to the implementor.

Auto mode is "execute reasonable assumptions on routine work," NOT "skip the
human checkpoints the methodology guarantees."

For every plan execution:
- One implementor per numbered plan step, fresh context each time. Do NOT
  reuse a single implementor across steps. Lettered sub-steps within one
  number share a single agent session.
- Run the affected tests after every plan completes; do not advance to the
  next module's plan until the current module is green.
- Project-wide green test suite is the contract for declaring a cycle done.

